---
import BlogPostLayout from '../../../../layouts/BlogPostLayout.astro';

const title = "Botany Career Options in India: 9 Routes, Plant Businesses and Real Pay";
const description =
  "Botany career options in India: nine routes from plant data and nurseries to NET and forests, with sourced contract pay (Rs 20,000 a month) and what scales";
const canonical = "https://futurecareerschool.com/blog/career-options/botany-career-options/";
const publishedDateISO = "2026-10-14";
const publishedDateLabel = "14 October 2026";
const authorName = "Shivanshi Sehgal";
const authorUrl = "https://futurecareerschool.com/about/shivanshi-sehgal/";
const authorRole = "Career researcher and writer";

const jumpLinks = [
  { id: "decoder", label: "What job ads call botany work" },
  { id: "routes", label: "Nine routes, ordered by growth room" },
  { id: "businesses", label: "Nursery, tissue culture, herbal: the business layers" },
  { id: "pay", label: "Government and research doors, with the sums" },
  { id: "proof", label: "Proof shelf and a first message" },
  { id: "ai", label: "Drones, satellites and AI in plant work" },
  { id: "money", label: "Fees, free learning and the MSc question" },
  { id: "checkpoint", label: "The 4-Checkpoint Protocol" },
  { id: "gates", label: "The 3 Gates" },
  { id: "mistakes", label: "Expensive wrong turns" },
  { id: "first-test", label: "Your first short test" },
  { id: "faq", label: "FAQs" },
];

const decoderRows = [
  { ad: "Taxonomist, herbarium or field botanist", branch: "Plant taxonomy: naming, collecting and mounting specimens", who: "Botanical Survey of India, universities, museum and botanic garden projects", note: "Many posts are project-based. Field travel is part of the job." },
  { ad: "Plant pathologist, plant health, seed testing", branch: "Plant pathology and mycology: diseases, fungi, seed health", who: "Seed and agri-input firms, testing labs, agricultural universities", note: "Lab and field mix. Closest to the crop-protection side of agriculture." },
  { ad: "Ecologist, biodiversity expert, vegetation survey", branch: "Plant ecology: sampling plots, species lists, habitat maps", who: "Environment consultancies, NGOs, forest and wildlife projects", note: "Report writing and mapping matter as much as plant knowledge." },
  { ad: "Raw drug identification, medicinal plant sourcing, quality officer", branch: "Ethnobotany and pharmacognosy: which plant, which part, how it is used", who: "Herbal and AYUSH makers, exporters, raw drug traders", note: "One wrong plant in a batch is a quality failure, so correct identification is the paid skill." },
  { ad: "Tissue culture officer, nursery manager, horticulture assistant", branch: "Plant physiology and propagation: growth, cuttings, culture media", who: "Nurseries, tissue culture units, floriculture and landscape firms", note: "Hands-on and repetitive. A business layer is easy to see here." },
  { ad: "Botany lecturer, PGT biology teacher", branch: "Any branch plus a teaching credential", who: "Schools, colleges, coaching and online platforms", note: "A fixed scale in a salaried post. A business layer sits outside it." },
];

const routeRows = [
  {
    route: "1. Plant data, mapping and science writing",
    scale: "high",
    scaleLabel: "Scalable",
    pays: "NGOs, consultants, research teams and publishers pay for a finished species list, a vegetation map or a clear report. They trust a public sample with dates and sources.",
    entry: "BSc or MSc Botany, plus free mapping software (QGIS), basic statistics and clear English. You can start without a licence or an exam.",
    layer: "Freelance mapping and survey analysis, then a small consulting service. Writers can add a newsletter. Skill sells remotely to many clients.",
  },
  {
    route: "2. Nursery, floriculture and landscape",
    scale: "mid",
    scaleLabel: "Scalable with a business layer",
    pays: "Homes, housing societies, farmers, institutions and landscapers. They trust healthy plants they can see and a neighbour who bought from you.",
    entry: "BSc Botany or Horticulture, plus time working in a live nursery. Salaried nursery jobs exist, but we found no sourced pay figures, so we print none.",
    layer: "Your own nursery, a plant-care subscription for offices and societies, or a landscape and garden service. Water, land and seasons are real costs.",
  },
  {
    route: "3. Tissue culture and planting material",
    scale: "mid",
    scaleLabel: "Scalable with a business layer",
    pays: "Nurseries and growers who need healthy, same-quality plants in bulk. They trust a batch record and independent test results.",
    entry: "BSc or MSc Botany plus a lab internship in culture media and sterile work. Employed lab roles come first.",
    layer: "A recognised production unit. The national scheme has a capacity bar and testing steps, set out in the next section.",
  },
  {
    route: "4. Herbal and AYUSH raw materials",
    scale: "mid",
    scaleLabel: "Scalable with a business layer",
    pays: "Herbal and AYUSH makers, exporters and traders. They trust correct plant identification, clean batches and written records.",
    entry: "MSc Botany with ethnobotany or pharmacognosy topics, plus raw drug identification. Quality roles at manufacturers are the usual start.",
    layer: "Supply of identified and tested raw material, contract cultivation with farmers, or a small branded product line. Licences differ by product and state, so verify them.",
  },
  {
    route: "5. Ecology and environment consulting",
    scale: "mid",
    scaleLabel: "Scalable with a business layer",
    pays: "Project developers who need environmental reports, and NGOs. They trust a field record, species lists and references from project leads.",
    entry: "MSc Botany or Ecology. Accredited consultancies field named experts by subject, including ecology and biodiversity, under the Quality Council of India NABET scheme. Check its current rules.",
    layer: "Ecological survey and biodiversity reports once you have a field record and a small team. See our environmental science guide for the wider field.",
  },
  {
    route: "6. Seed, crop protection and plant health",
    scale: "mid",
    scaleLabel: "Scalable with a business layer",
    pays: "Seed and agri-input companies, testing labs and farmers. They trust lab discipline and farm-level results.",
    entry: "BSc or MSc Botany with plant pathology or seed technology. The company and exam detail sits in our plant and agricultural biotechnology guide.",
    layer: "A plant-health advisory or testing service. Seed testing and sale rules differ, so check what needs a licence.",
  },
  {
    route: "7. Teaching and tutoring",
    scale: "mid",
    scaleLabel: "Scalable with a business layer",
    pays: "Schools and colleges on a fixed scale. Students and parents pay for tuition and online courses.",
    entry: "B.Ed for school classes; MSc plus B.Ed for senior classes; NET or a state eligibility test for college posts.",
    layer: "Small-batch tuition, a recorded course or a channel for one exam and one level. Results and demo classes build trust.",
  },
  {
    route: "8. Research and taxonomy posts",
    scale: "low",
    scaleLabel: "Not so scalable",
    pays: "Funding agencies and institutes. They trust papers, a supervisor and a project record.",
    entry: "MSc, then CSIR-UGC NET, a university test or a project post. Detail and pay are in the ledger below.",
    layer: "Keep a mapping, data or writing skill that sells outside the lab. Consulting is possible after you hold a niche.",
  },
  {
    route: "9. Forest service (IFS and state range officer)",
    scale: "low",
    scaleLabel: "Not so scalable",
    pays: "Central and state governments. The route is employer-only.",
    entry: "A degree naming Botany or a listed subject. The central exam and state notices set the rest.",
    layer: "None inside the post. Keep a second skill running through exam years. Sales skill matters mainly for the move after service or the side route.",
  },
];

const businessCards = [
  {
    tone: "teal",
    badge: "Business layer 1",
    title: "A nursery",
    sells: "Plants, plant care and garden set-up.",
    rules: "Local trade and water rules. Sale of some planting material may need registration, so ask your State Horticulture Mission office.",
    number: "Plants sold each month against plants lost. A nursery that loses too many plants is selling its own cost.",
    watch: "Opening before you have worked a full season in someone else's nursery.",
  },
  {
    tone: "gold",
    badge: "Business layer 2",
    title: "A tissue culture lab",
    sells: "Healthy, same-quality planting material in bulk to nurseries and growers.",
    rules: "The Department of Biotechnology runs the National Certification System for Tissue Culture Raised Plants. A DST notice says a production facility must be fully operational and have a minimum capacity of 5 lakh plants a year to be recognised, and recognised units get batches tested by accredited labs.",
    number: "Contamination rate per batch. It shows whether your sterile work is good enough to sell.",
    watch: "Buying equipment for a lab that cannot reach the capacity bar. Work in a recognised unit first.",
  },
  {
    tone: "violet",
    badge: "Business layer 3",
    title: "Herbal raw material and products",
    sells: "Correctly identified, clean medicinal plant material, or a small finished product.",
    rules: "Older government and trade summaries say more than 90 percent of traded medicinal species come from the wild, and cultivation is promoted with subsidy support through AYUSH schemes. Rules and schemes change, so read the current National AYUSH Mission guidance.",
    number: "Batches accepted on first test against batches returned. Repeat orders show trust.",
    watch: "Selling a plant you cannot identify with certainty. Authentication is the paid skill.",
  },
];

const payRows = [
  { route: "BSI Project Assistant (16 posts)", rule: "Bachelor's degree in Botany, Life Sciences, Sanskrit, Ayurveda or Environmental Sciences; age up to 50", pay: "Rs 20,000 a month plus house rent allowance (HRA), temporary, up to 2 years", sum: "20,000 x 12 = Rs 2,40,000 a year before HRA. These 16 posts are 64 percent of the 25 posts in the notice." },
  { route: "BSI Project Associate-I (8 posts)", rule: "Master's in Botany, Life Sciences, Ayurveda or Environmental Sciences, or MA Sanskrit; age up to 35", pay: "Rs 31,000 a month plus HRA, same project", sum: "31,000 x 12 = Rs 3,72,000 a year. This is 84 percent of the CSIR JRF stipend." },
  { route: "BSI Research Associate-II (1 post)", rule: "PhD in Botany, Life Sciences or Environmental Sciences; age up to 45", pay: "Rs 61,000 a month, consolidated", sum: "61,000 x 12 = Rs 7,32,000 a year, for one post, on a contract that ends with the project." },
  { route: "CSIR-UGC NET JRF, then SRF", rule: "Master's at 55 percent (50 percent for reserved categories); age up to 30 with relaxations", pay: "Rs 37,000 a month for two years, Rs 42,000 after", sum: "37,000 x 12 = Rs 4,44,000; 42,000 x 12 = Rs 5,04,000. A fellowship is training, not a post." },
  { route: "Andhra Pradesh Forest Range Officer (notification 16/2026)", rule: "A bachelor's degree in one of 14 listed subject groups, which include Botany, Horticulture, Environmental Science and Computer Science", pay: "Pay scale Rs 48,440 to Rs 1,37,220; 4 posts; applications 16 October to 5 November 2026", sum: "The lowest step, 48,440 x 12 = Rs 5,81,280 a year, before the scale grows. Four posts for a whole state." },
  { route: "Indian Forest Service", rule: "Bachelor's degree with Botany among the named subjects (or Agriculture, Forestry or Engineering); age 21 to 32 as reported", pay: "About 80 vacancies reported for 2026; mains from 22 November 2026", sum: "Verify every date on the UPSC notice. The service is long, and our biology guide has the full subject list." },
];

const checkpointItems = [
  { num: "01", title: "Biology: where do you work best?", body: "Picture a full day in each route: nursery beds in summer, a sterile hood, a forest plot, a classroom, or a laptop with maps. Which day could you repeat for years?", note: "Nursery and field work test stamina, culture work tests patience with repeats, and mapping tests focus." },
  { num: "02", title: "Context: what can your family carry?", body: "Count the thin months: a project contract that ends, a nursery between seasons, a lab that needs funding. How many such months can your home carry?", note: "Set any fee beside the pay rows in the ledger before you decide." },
  { num: "03", title: "Market: who actually pays?", body: "From the routes table, write down one real person or office that would pay for your route. No name means no route yet.", note: "A subject named on a notice does not prove there is demand." },
  { num: "04", title: "Survival: what if this gate closes?", body: "Write the backup route and the skill you would carry into it. Also write which task in your route apps and drones will take over.", note: "Plans that last do not depend on one seat or one project." },
];

const gateItems = [
  { num: "Gate 1", title: "Proof of Skill", body: "Finish one thing that someone can check: a photographed, dated plant list of a local patch, a cuttings trial with a survival count, or a small map." },
  { num: "Gate 2", title: "Proof of Communication", body: "Describe that work aloud in half a minute to two minutes, to a nursery owner or a farmer, in a home language and in English." },
  { num: "Gate 3", title: "Proof of Value", body: "Show it to three people already in the field, for example a nursery owner, a herbal quality officer and a project botanist. Ask what is useful and what is missing." },
];

const mistakes = [
  { num: "01", title: "Choosing MSc Botany for the title alone", body: "An MSc opens project posts, NET and teaching. It does not add pay unless you add a skill beside it. Name the route that needs the degree first." },
  { num: "02", title: "Treating a project post as a job", body: "The BSI notice says the posts are temporary and end with the project. Plan the next step from the first month." },
  { num: "03", title: "Buying lab equipment before working in a recognised unit", body: "A unit has to be fully running at a set capacity to be recognised. Learn what that takes from someone who runs one." },
  { num: "04", title: "Selling herbal material you cannot authenticate", body: "A look-alike plant in a batch ends trust with a customer. Learn identification before you sell anything." },
  { num: "05", title: "Opening a nursery with no plan to sell", body: "Plants grow on their own schedule and do not wait for customers. Decide who buys, and when, before you plant." },
  { num: "06", title: "Paying for a certificate with no lab hours", body: "Culture work is a hand skill. A course without supervised practice is a weak proof." },
];

const testSteps = [
  { title: "Pick two routes, not nine", body: "Run those two through the four checks and park the other seven." },
  { title: "Open the real notice or scheme page for each", body: "Read the eligibility, age limit and pay on the issuing office's own page." },
  { title: "Do one small piece of work in each", body: "A plant list of one patch, a cuttings trial, time helping at a nursery, or a small map of a green space nearby." },
  { title: "Ask three people who do the work", body: "Talk to two who stayed and one who left. Ask what year one was like and how they found their first paying client or employer." },
  { title: "Drop one route in writing", body: "Cannot let one go? Then you are still comparing titles, not work." },
];

const faqItems = [
  { q: "What are the career options in botany?", a: "Nine routes deserve a look: plant data and mapping; nurseries and landscape; tissue culture; herbal and AYUSH raw material; ecology consulting; seed and plant health; teaching; research via NET and project posts; and forest service. Compare them by who pays, the entry rule and the skill you add." },
  { q: "What can I do after BSc Botany?", a: "You can do an MSc, a B.Ed for school teaching, a project assistant post, a nursery or lab role, or build a skill such as QGIS mapping, plant identification or tissue culture. A BSI notice in 2025 asked only for a bachelor's degree in Botany or Life Sciences for 16 project assistant posts at Rs 20,000 a month plus HRA." },
  { q: "Is botany a good career in India?", a: "It works as a base when you add a second skill and a record of work. Alone, it mostly leads to contract, lab and teaching posts. Growth comes from mapping and data, a nursery, a tissue culture or herbal business, or consulting." },
  { q: "How much does a botanist earn in India?", a: "There is no reliable national figure. Sourced contract data: BSI project posts at Rs 20,000, Rs 31,000 and Rs 61,000 a month by qualification, and the CSIR JRF at Rs 37,000 a month. Career sites print ranges with no source. Check live postings and ask people in the route." },
  { q: "Can a botany graduate become an IFS officer?", a: "Yes. Botany is among the subjects named for the Indian Forest Service as reported for 2026, which listed about 80 vacancies. It is a long exam, so keep a second income path while you prepare, and confirm every rule on the UPSC notice." },
  { q: "Which NET paper does a botany student take?", a: "Botany graduates normally sit Life Sciences in CSIR-UGC NET. From the December 2026 cycle NTA has merged the DBT-BET into this paper and added a Life Sciences and Biotechnology discipline. Exam days were reported as 19, 22 and 27 December 2026 with the portal closing on 5 November. Check csirnet.nta.nic.in." },
  { q: "Can I start a plant nursery or tissue culture lab after BSc Botany?", a: "A nursery is the easier start, and it is best after a season working in one. A tissue culture business is harder because a unit must be fully running at a minimum capacity of 5 lakh plants a year to be recognised under the national scheme. Work in a recognised unit before you invest." },
  { q: "Is medicinal plant work a good option for botany students?", a: "It can be, if you can identify plants correctly and keep records. India's AYUSH and herbal exports were reported at over 688 million dollars in 2024-25, up from 649 million dollars. The business layer is supply, contract cultivation or a small product line, with licences that differ by product." },
  { q: "Will AI or drones replace botanists?", a: "AI plant identification apps and drone images are taking over routine sorting and basic survey work. They do not replace field judgment, lab skill or the trust of a grower. Learn to check what the tool says and keep a log of its errors." },
  { q: "Do I need an MSc in Botany?", a: "Take it when the route asks for it: NET, college teaching, BSI associate posts or ecology consulting. A nursery, mapping or writing route may need only the bachelor's degree plus proof of work. Check the cost against the 10 and 20 percent rule of thumb in the fees section, and skip a loan unless the credential is required." },
];

const sources = [
  { label: "Botanical Survey of India: advertisement for 25 project posts, Plant Based Wildlife Health Management (November 2025)", href: "https://bsi.gov.in/uploads/documents/whatsnew/hindi/Advertisement_for_Plant_based_wildlife_health_managment_2025.pdf" },
  { label: "CSIR-HRDG: revised JRF guidelines (stipend, eligibility, age)", href: "https://csirhrdg.res.in/Home/Index/1/Default/3384/60" },
  { label: "Careers360 News: DBT-BET merged into CSIR-UGC NET Life Sciences from December 2026", href: "https://news.careers360.com/nta-merges-dbt-bet-joint-csir-ugc-net-life-sciences-exam-from-december-2026-jrf-assistant-professor-phd-admissions-competitive" },
  { label: "Andhra Pradesh Public Service Commission: Forest Range Officer notification 16/2026", href: "https://psc.ap.gov.in/" },
  { label: "Careers360: UPSC IFS 2026 eligibility and vacancies (check the UPSC notice)", href: "https://competition.careers360.com/articles/upsc-ifs-2026" },
  { label: "PIB: Namo Drone Didi scheme (4 November 2024)", href: "https://static.pib.gov.in/WriteReadData/specificdocs/documents/2024/nov/doc2024114428601.pdf" },
  { label: "News On AIR: AYUSH and herbal exports, 2024-25", href: "https://www.newsonair.gov.in/health-minister-jp-nadda-reports-6-rise-in-ayush-and-herbal-exports" },
  { label: "Civilsdaily: Indian Virtual Herbarium of the Botanical Survey of India (August 2022)", href: "https://www.civilsdaily.com/news/indian-virtual-herbarium-biggest-database-of-countrys-flora-is-a-global-hit/" },
  { label: "Department of Science and Technology: National Certification System for Tissue Culture Raised Plants", href: "https://dst.gov.in/news/national-certification-system-tissue-culture-raised-plants" },
];

const faqJsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  mainEntity: faqItems.map((item) => ({
    "@type": "Question",
    name: item.q,
    acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: item.a },
  })),
};
---

<BlogPostLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical={canonical}
  category="career-options"
  publishedAt={publishedDateISO}
  publishedLabel={publishedDateLabel}
  authorName={authorName}
  authorUrl={authorUrl}
  authorRole={authorRole}
  jsonLdExtras={[faqJsonLd]}
>
  <p class="lead-para">
    <strong>Botany career options in India start with one plain fact: job ads rarely say "botany". They say horticulture, plant health, raw drug quality, ecology or nursery manager. The routes that grow past a fixed pay band sell a plant skill directly: plant data and mapping, a nursery, a tissue culture lab, herbal raw material and ecology consulting.</strong>
    Pair a botany degree with one of these and proof people can check, and it becomes a high-value skill portfolio that supports better income options and earlier financial freedom.
  </p>

  <div class="key-takeaways">
    <p class="key-takeaways__label">The short version</p>
    <ul>
      <li>In a 2025 Botanical Survey of India notice, 16 of 25 project posts needed only a bachelor's degree, paid Rs 20,000 a month plus HRA, and ended with the project. A botany degree alone starts on contract pay.</li>
      <li>The routes with the most room are plant data and mapping, then nursery, tissue culture, herbal raw material and ecology consulting. Research and forest service are Not so scalable and need a second skill beside them.</li>
      <li><strong>Your next step: choose two routes, open the official notice or scheme page for each, and finish one small piece of plant work in each before you pay for an MSc.</strong></li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <p>
    Three questions come up all the time: "what can I do after BSc botany?", "is botany a good career?" and "can a botany graduate become an IFS officer?". Most pages reply with a pile of job titles and pay that has no source. This guide names the payer for each route, the rule that sets entry, a business layer where one exists, and a scalability label.
  </p>
  <p>
    The plant and agricultural biotechnology side, with seed companies and ICAR exams, has its own guide: <a href="/blog/career-options/plant-and-agricultural-biotechnology-careers/">plant and agricultural biotechnology careers</a>. For the ecology and consulting side, read <a href="/blog/career-options/environmental-science-career-options/">environmental science career options</a>. More degree guides are in <a href="/blog/career-options/">career options</a>. If you want a second opinion on your shortlist, <a href="https://futurecareerschool.com/services/career-counselling-and-career-guidance/career-counselling-for-pcb-students/">career counselling for PCB students</a> looks at your marks, money and home situation together.
  </p>

  <nav class="jump-nav" aria-label="Article sections">
    <p class="jump-nav__label">Jump to section</p>
    <ul class="jump-nav__list">
      {jumpLinks.map((item) => (
        <li><a href={`#${item.id}`}>{item.label}</a></li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </nav>

  <h2 id="decoder">What job ads call botany work</h2>

  <p>
    A student who searches job boards for "botany" finds very little. The same skills sit under other names. Read the ad words in the left column, then check which branch of your degree they come from.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Ad words</th>
          <th>Botany branch behind it</th>
          <th>Who hires</th>
          <th>Daily reality</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {decoderRows.map((row) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Ad words"><strong>{row.ad}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="Botany branch behind it">{row.branch}</td>
            <td data-label="Who hires">{row.who}</td>
            <td data-label="Daily reality">{row.note}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="routes">Nine routes, ordered by growth room</h2>

  <p>
    We ranked these by how far a person can grow income and independence with skill and proof, not by how respected the title sounds. Plant data and mapping come first because one skill serves many clients. The plant businesses follow, since each has a real market and a layer you can own.
  </p>
  <p>
    Teaching sits in the middle. Research and forest service come last: each has a hard entry test and then a second, separate climb. "Scalable" here means your income and reach can keep growing through skills, consulting or a business you own.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table paths-table--routes">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Route and rating</th>
          <th>Who pays, and how they trust you</th>
          <th>How you enter</th>
          <th>Business layer</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {routeRows.map((row) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Route and rating"><strong>{row.route}</strong><br /><span class:list={["scale-pill", `scale-pill--${row.scale}`]}>{row.scaleLabel}</span></td>
            <td data-label="Who pays, and how they trust you">{row.pays}</td>
            <td data-label="How you enter">{row.entry}</td>
            <td data-label="Business layer">{row.layer}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      <strong>Scalable ownership inside botany:</strong> a nursery with a plant-care subscription, a recognised tissue culture unit, a supply line of identified herbal raw material, and a mapping and survey consulting service all let you set the price, add repeat customers and later hire people. A fixed salary does not. Each needs capital, a licence or clients, so put ownership after a season or two inside a working unit.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    If your real goal is faster salaried pay, look hard at two neighbours. Plant biotechnology has more company hooks, and environmental science reaches consulting sooner. Botany keeps its edge when you want plant identification, field ecology or herbal quality work, which those degrees teach less of.
  </p>

  <h2 id="businesses">Nursery, tissue culture, herbal: the business layers</h2>

  <p>
    Most botany pages stop at "start a nursery". Here is what each layer sells, what rules to check and the one number worth watching. We found no reliable public figures for set-up cost in these businesses, so we print none. Ask three people who run one.
  </p>

  <div class="lane-grid">
    {businessCards.map((c) => (
      <div class:list={["lane-card", `lane-card--${c.tone}`]}>
        <span class="lane-card__badge">{c.badge}</span>
        <div class="lane-card__title">{c.title}</div>
        <p><strong>What it sells:</strong> {c.sells}</p>
        <div class="lane-row lane-row--layer"><span>Rules to check</span> {c.rules}</div>
        <div class="lane-row lane-row--fit"><span>Number to watch</span> {c.number}</div>
        <div class="lane-row lane-row--watch"><span>Watch out</span> {c.watch}</div>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    The tissue culture scheme figures come from a Department of Science and Technology notice that reported about 80 recognised companies in November 2017, so the count is dated. Herbal figures come from government pages that mix years. Read the current scheme pages before you spend.
  </p>

  <h2 id="pay">Government and research doors, with the sums</h2>

  <p>
    A monthly figure tells you little until you see how long the contract runs and how many posts exist. Each row below comes from a notice or an official page. We skipped the unsourced pay ranges that career sites print for the same degree.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table paths-table--money">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Door</th>
          <th>Entry rule</th>
          <th>Pay signal</th>
          <th>The sum</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {payRows.map((row) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Door"><strong>{row.route}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="Entry rule">{row.rule}</td>
            <td data-label="Pay signal">{row.pay}</td>
            <td data-label="The sum">{row.sum}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    Pay figures here are indicative ranges and averages from public notices, not exact or guaranteed amounts. Pay differs by city, employer, skill and year. Check current job listings and ask people working in the role before you decide. Contract notices are for the dates shown and can change.
  </p>

  <div class="callout callout--amber">
    <p class="callout__eyebrow">Two filters, not one</p>
    <ul class="callout__list">
      <li>Research has a hard entry stage (NET, then a funded seat) and a separate hard advancement stage (a post that is open). Clearing the first does not clear the second.</li>
      <li>A BSI project post is temporary by its own terms. A permanent post is a different recruitment, so a project post is training and a reference, not a seat on a ladder.</li>
      <li>Forest service has an exam stage and then very few posts. Four range officer posts in a whole state shows the size of the pool.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      The BSI notice asked for Botany, Ethnobotany, Ayurveda, plant taxonomy and Sanskrit, and accepted an MA in Sanskrit for the associate posts. That tells you where the field is moving: botany joined to classical texts, field travel and data compilation. A botany student who learns to read plant names across languages and records field notes cleanly is rarer than one more MSc holder.
    </p>
    <p>
      From the December 2026 cycle the DBT-BET test is merged into the CSIR-UGC NET Life Sciences paper, which adds a Life Sciences and Biotechnology discipline. Read the NTA bulletin for the paper that suits a botany degree before you enrol anywhere.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="proof">Proof shelf and a first message</h2>

  <p>
    <strong>The skill portfolio decides the route, not the degree name:</strong> one core skill (plant identification, culture work or field survey), one multiplier (QGIS, statistics, English and a local language, simple selling) and proof someone can check.
  </p>

  <ol class="rich-list">
    <li><strong>A dated plant list.</strong> Fifty or more plants from one campus or village patch, each with a photo, place and date, saved on a public platform. It is proof of identification skill.</li>
    <li><strong>Ten pressed and labelled specimens.</strong> Collected with care, named, and shown in photos. Ask a teacher to check the names.</li>
    <li><strong>A cuttings or germination log.</strong> Same conditions, a survival count and one line on what went wrong. A nursery owner reads this fast.</li>
    <li><strong>A culture contamination log.</strong> If you do lab work, record each batch and its failure rate. It shows honesty, which a unit owner values.</li>
    <li><strong>A small map and a one-page note.</strong> A free GIS map of a green space with species zones and what you would protect.</li>
  </ol>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Growth moves that decide the outcome</p>
    <p>
      Choosing the plant skill that a paying customer already wants is the decision that unlocks high income opportunities. Then stack three things on the degree. Mapping or data skill makes you hireable by consultants. Clear English, written and spoken, wins project posts, grants and clients. Show your plant log on one public platform instead of five, because that is where a nursery owner, a herbal quality head or a project lead will find you.
    </p>
    <p>
      Meet people who do the work: a nursery owner, a herbal quality officer, a project botanist. One useful question and a follow-up beat a vague request for guidance. Mapping, writing and analysis can be done from anywhere, so a smaller city is not a barrier. Nursery, lab and field routes need you on site, so choose with your work style in mind. One number to watch for any route: enquiries that turn into paid work or a second interview.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="script-box">
    <p class="script-box__label">A first message to a nursery owner</p>
    <p>
      "I studied BSc Botany and I keep a dated plant log of our street. I noticed your jasmine cuttings look healthy in the front row. Could I spend an afternoon helping with records, and share a one-page note on which plants survive best? I will not take your time for more than that."
    </p>
    <p>
      It names one specific thing, offers one piece of proof and asks one small question. The first sales skill for this field is simple: say who you help, show proof, follow up once, and ask for a name of someone else who might need the help.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="ai">Drones, satellites and AI in plant work</h2>

  <p>
    Government programmes now put drones on farms. Namo Drone Didi planned drones for 15,000 women self-help groups over 2024-25 and 2025-26. It covers 80 percent of the drone and accessory cost up to Rs 8 lakh and trains one member for 15 days, with 5 days of pilot training and 10 days on nutrient and pesticide application.
  </p>
  <p>
    The scheme is for self-help groups, not graduates, and its listed period has ended, so check whether it was extended. It still shows where plant knowledge meets a paid task: someone must read the field and decide what to spray and where.
  </p>
  <p>
    Satellite maps matter too. The India State of Forest Report 2023, as summarised by exam-prep sites, put forest and tree cover at 25.17 percent of the country, and about 37 percent of that cover lies outside recorded forest areas. Mapping trees on farms and village land is a real task that needs plant knowledge and basic remote sensing.
  </p>
  <p>
    On the lab side, the Botanical Survey of India holds more than 30 lakh herbarium specimens, and its Indian Virtual Herbarium had about one lakh specimen records as of August 2022, roughly 1 in 30. Digitising that backlog is long work, so watch the BSI vacancy page for such projects.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Task</th>
          <th>AI or drone pressure</th>
          <th>What stays human</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Task"><strong>Naming common plants from photos</strong></td>
          <td data-label="AI or drone pressure">High. Apps do it in seconds for common species.</td>
          <td data-label="What stays human">Look-alike species, rare plants and confirming a name before a batch or a report depends on it.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Task"><strong>Herbarium data entry</strong></td>
          <td data-label="AI or drone pressure">High. Label reading and sorting are being automated.</td>
          <td data-label="What stays human">Correct naming, checking odd labels, and field collection.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Task"><strong>Crop and plot surveys by drone</strong></td>
          <td data-label="AI or drone pressure">Medium. Images replace some walking.</td>
          <td data-label="What stays human">Ground checks, deciding what the pattern means, and talking to the grower.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Task"><strong>Tissue culture and nursery handwork</strong></td>
          <td data-label="AI or drone pressure">Low for now.</td>
          <td data-label="What stays human">Sterile technique, plant judgment and sales.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    Start now: learn QGIS, check every app answer against a flora or a teacher, and keep a log of what the tool got wrong.
  </p>
  <p>
    Later, as tools and adoption mature, build a small workflow, such as a survey template that turns drone or satellite images into a plot-level note, and show it. No multiplier is guaranteed. AI lets you take on more survey or batch work in the same hours. It does not replace the plant knowledge.
  </p>

  <h2 id="money">Fees, free learning and the MSc question</h2>

  <p>
    Use a strict rule of thumb, not a law: spend about 10 percent of the whole education budget on college, and treat 20 percent as the point where you need proof for that exact course. On a Rs 8 lakh budget that is Rs 80,000 and Rs 1.6 lakh. The remaining money buys a laptop, a camera, field travel, rent between contracts and a second skill.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      A degree is the licence for a lab, a notice or a teaching post. Plant identification, QGIS, statistics and clear writing are all learnable from good YouTube channels, floras, herbarium sites, scheme pages and cheap courses. Pay only when you get feedback or a credential an employer asks for. Ten certificates prove little. A year of dated plant notes proves more.
    </p>
    <p>
      Tissue culture and herbal quality work are hand skills. You need supervised lab hours, so choose a college or unit for its bench time. Before you pay for any short course, check who teaches it, when it was last updated, how many supervised bench hours it gives, the refund terms and what you will have made at the end. Avoid an education loan unless a credential is legally required.
    </p>
  </div>

